{"nodes": [{"id": "thunder_laser_nova35_device_thunder_laser_nova_35", "label": "Thunder Laser Nova 35 Device Profile", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "rayforge/resources/devices/thunder-laser-nova35/device.yaml", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "thunder_laser_nova35_device_ruidadriver", "label": "RuidaDriver", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "rayforge/resources/devices/thunder-laser-nova35/device.yaml", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Ruida controllers speak a proprietary binary UDP protocol on two ports (main_port 50200 for job data, jog_port 50210 for manual motion) instead of textual G-code. This is why the Thunder Laser Nova 35 is the only device folder in this chunk with no dialect.yaml: there is no G-code template layer to configure."}, {"id": "thunder_laser_nova35_device_top_left_origin", "label": "Top-Left Origin Convention", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "rayforge/resources/devices/thunder-laser-nova35/device.yaml", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Ruida-controlled CO2 cutters home to the rear-left and count Y downward, so the Nova 35 is the lone top_left profile here. Declaring origin per device is what lets the same toolpath geometry be emitted correctly for both families."}, {"id": "thunder_laser_nova35_device_co2_head_pwm_profile", "label": "CO2 Head PWM Profile", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "rayforge/resources/devices/thunder-laser-nova35/device.yaml", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The only head in this chunk that carries laser_type: co2 plus pwm_frequency, max_pwm_frequency and pulse-width bounds. CO2 tubes are driven by pulse timing rather than the simple 0-1000 S-word scale diode heads 